# Vault entry recycle and recovery

This source-assisted bounded slice implements single-entry native recycling, read-only masked bin preview, recovery to an existing previous parent group and confirmed permanent deletion. It is a partial KeePass2.61.1 contract. Application actions execute native JavaScript; they do not launch KeePass or import the original assembly.

Normal Delete moves the complete entry to the UUID-designated bin when recycling is enabled. New local vaults enable recycling by default. Imported native settings are honored: disabled recycling, an entry already inside the bin or its descendants, or an explicit permanent choice removes the entry and records its native UUID/time tombstone. A missing bin is created beneath the root with trash icon43, searching/Auto-Type disabled and expanded state. Recovery requires an existing, different previous parent outside the bin. UUID, represented content, protected flags, history, attachment references and unknown native XML are retained. Delete updates access/usage without adding a history revision; recovery updates placement/location and records the old bin as the new previous parent.

The active list excludes bin entries. Editing, history and TOTP actions reject bin entries. Show bin exposes a separate private preview which begins masked. Recovery and permanent purge require confirmation. New recycle commands require a private-key/whole-native-graph-bound revision; represented database, attachment or key changes invalidate it. The legacy confirmed ID-only `delete` alias remains accepted without stale-selection protection. These guards are Studio policies, not claims about an original KeePass API.

Client publication checks view and bin generations after awaited active-list refresh. Closing/reopening the bin or choosing a new preview prevents an older mutation refresh from reopening it or clearing that preview. Existing dirty-editor, enabled-field pending-draft, selection, lock and disposal safeguards remain in place. The exact correction comprises two post-list `viewCurrent()`→`binCurrent()` checks; backend source bytes are unchanged from the original recycle candidate.

## Released behavior and deliberate limits

The independent oracle is the exact KeePass2.61.1 portable assembly SHA256 `04d29636c393f0c1fc99ff739295494be3adefded93271e44591e8e300225a5a`. The source carrier is5,333,853 bytes with SHA256 `711bd9ec7076661678469e51bae876debac07a08c32f1b1c6b3b8fe5e761c13a`; its1381-entry census and16 selected source/license files are retained as finite provenance. Publisher digest matching was recorded; OpenPGP signature verification is not claimed. See [RECYCLE-NOTICE.md](RECYCLE-NOTICE.md).

The released writer chooses KDBX4.0 for the default tested feature set and omits PreviousParentGroup on save/reopen. The lifecycle oracle uses a real QualityCheck=false feature to select4.1; candidate4.1 exports retain the previous-parent reference and the original4.1 reader reads it. A later original save without a4.1-only feature can downgrade and lose the reference again. The default4.0 loss fixture remains separate; conditional4.1 equality must not be reported as default-release lifecycle equality.

Delete/restore orchestration and fixed clock assignments in the comparison are composed from authenticated released source. The actual library performs database New/Open/Save, collection removal/AddEntry, CreateBackup/Touch, tombstone construction and the private static EnsureRecycleBin helper in finite fixtures. Original GUI handlers execute0 times. Exact original wall-clock/event/dialog behavior is not established. The unused bin expiry differs: released construction uses process-global PwDefs.DtDefaultNow, candidate construction uses the operation clock; Expires=false in both. Raw unadapted expiry observations are retained.

The slice bounds preserved XML plus attachment base64 text at8MiB; entries, groups and tombstones at10,000 each; XML nodes at100,000; XML depth64 and group depth63. It checks capacity before bin creation, uses uint64 usage arithmetic and accepts strict UTC clocks in years0001–9999 with seconds or three fractional digits. Incoming API data is privately captured before queued awaits with12MiB text/key bytes,100,000 nodes and depth64. Existing KDBX/KDF bounds apply. These are accepted input bounds, not process-memory isolation or preemption of blocked I/O.

Group deletion/recovery, whole-bin emptying, multiple selected entries, arbitrary group movement, bin settings/localization/collapse configuration and general previous-group recovery outside the bin remain absent. Root-as-bin malformed metadata is rejected. Full XML/schema validation, synchronization, broader history deletion/age policies, expiration workflows, Auto-Type, providers and other manual/product contracts remain open. Full compatibility, full product parity, security parity and superiority are unestablished.

## Separate evidence epochs

The previously accepted current-layout history campaign remains41 focused groups and24 original assembly loads. Its sources/receipt stay historical. Original recycle author68 groups comprise prior41 plus new27 (12 native,5 server,4 DOM,2 HTTP,4 original groups);38 assembly loads comprise prior24 plus new14. That campaign used the older client. The initial independent review has11 groups:8 passed and3 confirmed stale bin publication failures, plus4 additional original library executions and one normal private browser acceptance. Those failures are preserved.

The two-guard correction author campaign passes38 frontend groups with0 original/HTTP/browser executions. A failed same-generation preview diagnostic had an invalid stale-token premise; raw/source records remain, with a passing explicit stale-token control. It is not another confirmed application defect. The final independent review passes the unchanged five boundary bodies plus two unchecked-confirmation controls (7/7), and one normal private-browser recovery/history/lock/restart acceptance; original executions in this campaign are0. Counts from these epochs must not be added as one unique corpus or rebound to new production bytes.

